window.TIMELINE = {"pace":{"wpm":170,"settle":3},"states":[{"id":"cover","label":"Opening","act":"Part I · The problem and the apparatus","heading":"Collective evidence assurance","duration":146,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"My background is in social networks and collective behavior. I am a behavioral and computational scientist, and a question that has followed me across much of my work is how information held by individuals becomes a belief or decision held by a group. We often talk about a group's knowledge as though it were simply the sum of what its members know. But between the two lies communication. Someone selects what to tell someone else. Another person interprets it, omits part of it, or reports a conclusion without the observations that supported it. The network through which those reports travel can change what the group is in a position to know.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":42.2,"text":"I started studying these questions in human communication networks. In experimental work with Matthew Brashears, we found a particularly instructive complication: attempts to correct a transmitted message could improve fidelity yet generate additional mutated versions of that message as it traveled. An intervention designed to repair an error could change the distribution of information circulating through the network (Brashears & Gladstone, 2016). That study concerned human diffusion of textual information, not probabilistic inference by machines. The continuity is a question about what communication does to information, rather than a claim that the two settings operate by the same mechanism.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":80.1,"text":"More recently, I have built controlled networks of language-model agents. I can assign each agent an observation, specify who can exchange messages, vary what those messages contain, and record the interaction as it unfolds. Because the underlying task has a known correct answer, I can compare what the agents collectively conclude with what their distributed evidence warrants. Those controls make some explanations testable that would be difficult to separate in a natural organization or deployed system.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":109.9,"text":"The results raise a question I want to pursue beyond the experiments themselves: whether interacting agents can become confidently wrong because communication changes what evidence reaches them or how they respond to it, and, more demanding still, whether early signs of that failure predict risk in networks, models, and tasks we have not examined. This talk moves from the findings that motivate the question to a proposed program, Collective Evidence Assurance, intended to test it. The stage distinguishes results from illustrative models and untested hypotheses as we go.","kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["brashears2016error"]},{"id":"why-system","label":"The unit of analysis","act":"Part I · The problem and the apparatus","heading":"Evaluating each agent is not the same as evaluating the system they form","duration":128,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Suppose a language model performs well when tested alone. Place several instances of that model in a network and the object being evaluated changes. Each instance has its own information and history of messages. Some encounter strong observations, some weak ones, and some encounter only another agent's summary. They may be individually competent at interpreting their own evidence yet reach a poor collective judgment because important information never reaches the decision point or because what reaches it is treated inappropriately.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":31.2,"text":"There are several places where such a failure might arise. An agent could misunderstand the observation assigned to it. A sender could strip away the strength or qualifications attached to an otherwise useful observation. A recipient could give an intact report more influence than it warrants. Several apparently corroborating messages might all descend from one observation. Those explanations need not be mutually exclusive, but they are not interchangeable. A problem of source dependence calls for different evidence and a different remedy than a problem of message compression or recipient updating. The duplicated-source case is a planned experiment, not a result from the studies I will show.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":71.6,"text":"This connects to a long tradition of social learning. Informational-cascade models examine what happens when people learn from others' choices without seeing the observations behind those choices (Bikhchandani et al., 1992). Models of networked belief aggregation ask when repeated interpersonal updating preserves or misrepresents information dispersed across a population (DeGroot, 1974; Golub & Jackson, 2010). These models establish intellectual antecedents, not a ready-made explanation of how language-model agents behave. My empirical question is whether communication-induced failures in agent networks have measurable signatures and whether those signatures travel beyond the configurations in which they were discovered.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":108.2,"text":"That last distinction matters throughout. Explaining a failure after seeing its outcome can reveal a mechanism worth investigating. Predicting the failure from information available before the final judgment, in a new configuration, requires evidence that a retrospective explanation does not provide.","kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["bikhchandani1992theory","degroot1974reaching","golub2010naive"]},{"id":"apparatus","label":"The experimental system","act":"Part I · The problem and the apparatus","heading":"Controlled networks make distinct explanations testable","duration":126,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"I use a controlled inference task with two possible states of the world. A controller assigns each agent a private observation and fixes how informative that observation is about the correct state. It also specifies the communication graph, what an agent is allowed to transmit, and how many rounds of exchange will occur. After each round, the agents report probabilistic beliefs. A pooling rule turns those reports into a group-level readout, which I can compare with the answer and with reference calculations based on the assigned evidence.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":33.7,"text":"The apparatus exists to make informative comparisons. If the same agents receive the same private observations under the same graph, while one condition permits evidence-bearing messages and another permits only verdicts, the difference in collective performance can be attributed to that communication-content treatment under the design. When I deliberately change a planted verdict and hold the rest of the initial evidence constant, I can estimate the effect of that intervention. Neither comparison, by itself, tells me the private computation a model performed when it read a message.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":67.4,"text":"Complete logs make the work inspectable. We know which message arrived, when it arrived, and which beliefs were subsequently reported. They allow targeted analysis and follow-up interventions. A temporal trace alone does not establish that a particular message caused a later failure, and recreating a stochastic configuration does not guarantee identical model outputs. That is why controlled contrasts, replications, and explicit reference rules matter alongside the logs.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":94.1,"text":"These advantages also mark the boundary of the laboratory. In a deployed system the original evidence may be hidden, its reliability uncertain, and the true answer unavailable until long after a decision. The experimenter can calculate reference quantities that no operational predictor could simply look up. CEA must use those references to learn and test the science while evaluating any eventual risk forecast using only what the system could observe when the forecast was made.","kind":"schematic"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"votes-vs-weight","label":"Counting versus weighing","act":"Part II · What the experiments show","heading":"Counting agents and weighing their evidence can favor different answers","duration":86,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Consider five agents assessing whether state A or state B is true. Three receive weak observations favoring B, while two receive stronger observations favoring A. Ask each agent for a verdict and count the answers, and B wins three to two. Combine the five observations according to their stipulated diagnostic strengths, and A wins. The visual uses simple weights to make the disagreement visible. These are illustrative values, not estimated psychological or model parameters.","kind":"toy"},{"at":29.1,"text":"The task is built this way for a reason. If the numerical majority and the better-supported answer always agreed, a group could produce the correct verdict through several different procedures and we could learn little about how it used the evidence. By putting a weak majority against a better-informed minority, the experiment creates a case in which counting verdicts and evaluating underlying observations make different predictions. The known likelihood information supplies a reference for judging the final answer.","kind":"toy"},{"at":59.6,"text":"This design does not reveal a recipient's internal rule. A wrong group decision could reflect disproportionate influence from many weak verdicts, mistaken interpretation of a strong signal, difficulties processing long messages, or the way beliefs are aggregated at the end. The experiment is useful precisely because those explanations can then be examined rather than inferred from the final vote alone.","kind":"toy"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"content-effect","label":"Evidence or conclusions","act":"Part II · What the experiments show","heading":"Sending conclusions in place of evidence lowered collective accuracy in two of three model families","duration":106,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"The first comparison changed what agents were allowed to communicate, while holding fixed the private evidence and the fully connected five-agent graph. The agents exchanged messages over three rounds. In one condition, a message carried the underlying observation and its strength. In the other, it carried the sender's conclusion without the evidence supporting it. The distinction resembles what happens when a detailed report becomes a recommendation or a vote. The recipient still hears an answer but cannot examine the sender's grounds on the same terms.","kind":"measured"},{"at":33,"text":"That change mattered. With gpt-4.1-mini, collective accuracy was 0.90 when evidence was shared and 0.64 when conclusions alone were shared, with 100 trials per condition. With claude-sonnet-4-6, the corresponding accuracies were 0.96 and 0.62, with 50 trials per condition (Gladstone, 2026a). The rings and solid discs on the stage indicate which kind of content each message carried. In claude-sonnet-4-6, 18.8 percent of conclusion messages still disclosed some evidence, which works against the observed difference. The group-level performance difference identifies a consequence of the allowed communication content in these conditions. It does not tell us whether recipients literally counted the verdicts or how they represented their evidentiary force.","kind":"measured"},{"at":73.8,"text":"There was also a limit to the pattern. Gemini-2.5-flash was the only family run with an 8,192-token thinking budget, which it needed to pass screening, and it kept 0.93 accuracy under conclusion sharing. The model family and reasoning budget therefore changed together. The finding is not a universal property of conclusion sharing. It is a substantial treatment contrast observed in two of the three tested families and a reason to investigate why the boundary differs elsewhere.","kind":"measured"}],"sources":["gladstone2026accuracy"]},{"id":"dispersion","label":"Dispersion","act":"Part II · What the experiments show","heading":"Under conclusion sharing, outcomes spread toward both extremes","duration":118,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"A drop in accuracy might suggest a familiar picture of social influence: agents repeatedly hear the misleading majority and converge on its wrong answer. The distribution of terminal beliefs makes that account too simple. Under conclusion sharing in gpt-4.1-mini, the mean pooled probability assigned to the true state changed little, from 0.632 initially to 0.643 at the end. Across trials, however, the standard deviation increased to 0.381. Outcomes accumulated near both ends of the probability scale (Gladstone, 2026a).","kind":"measured"},{"at":30.5,"text":"The plotted curves show the empirical cumulative distributions across 100 trials per condition. The horizontal axis is the final pooled probability of the true state. Left of 0.5, the group favored the wrong answer. Thirty-six conclusion-sharing trials ended on that side, compared with ten evidence-sharing trials. But many other conclusion-sharing trials ended very confident in the correct answer. Looking only at average probability or at the proportion correct obscures how strongly the outcomes differed from one run to the next.","kind":"measured"},{"at":61.8,"text":"There is a second distribution that the figure does not show. Within trials, agents' beliefs also became more diverse under conclusion sharing. This is not evidence that every group remained divided, but it contradicts a simple account in which the principal pattern is uniform convergence on one false consensus. The terminal group belief is calculated using a logarithmic opinion pool, and the frequency of confidently wrong outcomes is lower under arithmetic pooling. The operational definition of a collective judgment therefore matters to how severe the tails appear.","kind":"measured"},{"at":95.5,"text":"The next question is not simply whether communication can make things worse. We have evidence that it can under particular conditions. The harder question is why otherwise comparable runs can end in such different places and whether some feature of early communication predicts which path a run will take.","kind":"measured"}],"sources":["gladstone2026accuracy"]},{"id":"verdict-response","label":"Response to verdicts","act":"Part II · What the experiments show","heading":"Agents crossed from correct to incorrect decisions more often than a confidence-weighted benchmark predicted","duration":107,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"A separate analysis followed agents whose private evidence initially favored the true state. It used 11-agent conclusion-sharing networks in gpt-4.1-mini and assembled 1,500 agent-trials across six levels of fan-in, meaning the number of neighbors whose verdicts an agent received. The design asks a more local question than whether the group was accurate: after reading neighboring verdicts, how often a correct agent abandoned the correct side of the decision boundary.","kind":"measured"},{"at":27.4,"text":"The comparison used a stated benchmark. It pooled the agent's private belief with first-round neighbor verdicts, weighted by the confidence reported with each verdict and treating those verdicts as independent for that calculation. Under this rule, the combined information crossed to the wrong side in 0.1 percent of cases. Agents' terminal beliefs actually crossed in 26.9 percent. Across the six fan-in cells, the excess was strongly associated with the number of neighbors, with a cell-level correlation of 0.91 (Gladstone, 2026a). The two percentages describe binary decision crossings, not the size of a belief update. The correlation is over six aggregate cells, not a causal estimate of a mechanism within an agent.","kind":"measured"},{"at":69.5,"text":"The benchmark is not the uniquely correct posterior under every possible message-generation process. Nevertheless, it asks a discriminating question. A correct agent was much more likely to abandon its answer than this confidence-sensitive rule predicted, and the discrepancy rose from about 0.08 at fan-in 2 to about 0.4 at fan-in 6 and above. That pattern motivates a study of how agent collectives assign influence to received verdicts. It does not establish that the models used a literal head-counting algorithm, nor does it supply the continuous distortion measure proposed later in the talk.","kind":"measured"}],"sources":["gladstone2026accuracy"]},{"id":"planted","label":"Planted verdict","act":"Part II · What the experiments show","heading":"Changing one planted verdict from correct to incorrect moved the group's final belief","duration":94,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"The previous analysis compared observed decisions with a benchmark. To test whether communicated verdicts could actively move the collective outcome, I used a more direct manipulation. The five-agent conclusion-sharing network received the same private evidence, while one confident first-mover verdict was planted. In one condition the planted verdict favored the true state, in another it favored the wrong state, and a third condition had no planted verdict.","kind":"measured"},{"at":26.6,"text":"Replacing the correct plant with the incorrect one reduced final collective probability on the true state by 0.51 in gpt-4.1-mini, across 40 trials, and by 0.64 in claude-sonnet-4-6, across 15 trials (Gladstone, 2026a). Those estimates contrast the correct and incorrect plants. Relative to no plant, an incorrect plant reduced final probability by approximately 0.25 and 0.40, respectively. The distinction among these contrasts matters because the no-plant condition is not the counterfactual represented by the larger numbers.","kind":"measured"},{"at":56.5,"text":"The intervention supports a causal conclusion about the direction of a communicated verdict in this experimental setting. It shows that one early message can move the group's final stated belief even when the assigned private evidence remains fixed. It does not identify the internal steps by which that happens, and it does not test whether one original observation gains undue influence when relayed through several paths. Those are different claims. The modest numbers of trials, especially in the second model family, also make replication and uncertainty estimation important before extending the result.","kind":"measured"}],"sources":["gladstone2026accuracy"]},{"id":"boundaries","label":"Boundaries","act":"Part II · What the experiments show","heading":"The effect depends on connectivity and on the model","duration":108,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"It would be tempting to conclude that agents should simply share full evidence instead of conclusions. The wider experiment rules out that general prescription. In 11-agent gpt-4.1-mini networks at the highest tested fan-in, evidence sharing was less accurate than conclusion sharing. With a misleading majority, accuracy was 0.38 rather than 0.58. When every private reading favored the truth, it was 0.68 rather than 0.96 (Gladstone, 2026a). These reversals occurred in a particular model and connectivity regime, not across every configuration.","kind":"measured"},{"at":31.2,"text":"One possible explanation is that evidence-bearing messages impose a greater processing burden. They are longer, and a highly connected recipient receives more of them. Across the fan-in ladder, sender count, incoming text volume, and aspects of path length changed together. At a fixed fan-in, evidence messages also differ from conclusions in length, evidentiary complexity, and provenance. None of these was independently manipulated. We cannot identify the source of the reversal by observing its association with fan-in alone. To separate those explanations, the follow-up research would need to vary the load, the number of informants, and the communication paths more independently.","kind":"measured"},{"at":69.5,"text":"The participating model and its reasoning budget also affect the outcome. Within claude-sonnet-4-6, extended private reasoning raised conclusion-sharing accuracy from 0.62 to 1.00, at approximately 3.3 times the cost per agent. Gemini's high conclusion-sharing accuracy may reflect something similar, but its model family and reasoning budget are confounded. The practical implication is not that one communication mode is safe. It is that a system-level reliance claim has to specify its model, communication structure, message type, and processing conditions. CEA asks whether the risk can eventually be estimated more generally than one experiment at a time.","kind":"measured"}],"sources":["gladstone2026accuracy"]},{"id":"what-not-established","label":"What the data do not show","act":"Part II · What the experiments show","heading":"The experiments identify communication effects, not a general predictive mechanism","duration":81,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"The studies support several claims at different levels. Changing permitted message content affected collective accuracy in two model families. Changing the direction of one planted verdict affected the group's final belief. These are experimental comparisons with controlled treatments. A separate recipient-level analysis found many more crossings from correct to incorrect decisions than a specified confidence-weighted benchmark predicted, with a strong association across fan-in cells. That last finding describes a behavioral discrepancy. It does not identify the rule used internally to make those decisions.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":32.3,"text":"Three further propositions have not been established. The experiments did not manipulate several messages derived from one original observation. They did not calculate and validate a continuous measure of evidentiary weight distortion. And they did not demonstrate that any risk predictor trained in one set of agent configurations forecasts confident collective error in substantially different graphs, model families, or tasks.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":56.5,"text":"This separation directs the proposed experiments. A causal effect of message content justifies testing how content transformations alter available evidence. A large benchmark discrepancy justifies testing alternative models of recipient updating. Neither warrants presenting duplicated sources or transportable error prediction as demonstrated mechanisms. These are the hypotheses on which the proposed program would stand or fall.","kind":"schematic"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"duplication","label":"Repeated relay","act":"Part III · The proposed science","heading":"A planned test separates independent sources from one source relayed many times","duration":104,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Imagine a recipient receiving four reports that favor the same answer. The reports could represent four separate observations, each adding new information. Or all four could descend from one original observation that was repeated through different routes. The recipient sees four arrivals in either case, but the amount of independent evidence is different. Agreement among messages is not itself proof of corroboration. This is the problem often described as circular reporting, here formulated as a controlled experiment rather than as a finding.","kind":"hypothesis"},{"at":31.9,"text":"The central manipulation would vary the dependence among source origins while keeping the recipient's exposure comparable. Message count, apparent signal strength, task, model, timing, and routing opportunities should be matched or deliberately crossed as factors. One cannot simply stipulate that four independent draws will have identical realized content in every run, so the design must specify how message distributions are yoked and how any content differences are handled. Otherwise an observed effect could be due to stronger messages, different paths, or greater attention rather than repeated provenance itself.","kind":"hypothesis"},{"at":66,"text":"Nothing in the collective-accuracy experiments tested this case. The agents' initial private observations were independent draws. A related manuscript examines a narrower measurement question: whether sets of messages reveal traces of shared drafting ancestry. In controlled settings, some shared ancestry can be recovered from collections of texts, though individual messages provide limited information (Gladstone, 2026b). Shared textual ancestry is not the same thing as independent acquisition of the underlying evidence. The proposed work would examine whether provenance dependence can be detected and whether it changes the relation between received messages and warranted belief.","kind":"hypothesis"}],"sources":["gladstone2026residue"]},{"id":"distortion","label":"Distortion","act":"Part III · The proposed science","heading":"The proposed measure compares three belief updates on a common scale","duration":148,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"The program proposes to define and validate a measure of evidentiary weight distortion. To see what it would mean, start with an agent's stated belief after its private observation but before it receives any messages. Express subsequent changes on a common log-odds scale oriented toward the true state. This common starting point matters because otherwise a difference between an observed posterior and a reference update could combine unlike quantities. The true state is known in the experiment and used for scientific scoring, not assumed to be known by a deployed predictor.","kind":"hypothesis"},{"at":35.1,"text":"There are three candidate updates. The oracle update is what would follow from counting each distinct original observation in the relevant ancestry once, with its known diagnostic strength. The message-conditioned update is what would be warranted by the messages actually available to the recipient, under a declared and testable model of how those messages were produced and how they depend on one another. The observed update is the change in the agent's reported belief. All three are differences from the same pre-message belief.","kind":"hypothesis"},{"at":67.4,"text":"The oracle-minus-message-conditioned difference describes the part of the full-information update not available through the received messages, conditional on the reference model. The observed-minus-message-conditioned difference is the candidate distortion measure. With a truth-oriented sign convention, a negative value means the agent updated less toward the truth, or more away from it, than the message-conditioned model warranted. It does not necessarily mean that the agent's belief actually moved toward the wrong answer. An absolute gap or a decomposition by evidence direction might serve a different scientific purpose, so the measures would have to be compared rather than chosen for mathematical convenience.","kind":"hypothesis"},{"at":105.4,"text":"The message-conditioned reference is not simply sitting in the data waiting to be read off. It depends on assumptions about message generation, evidentiary dependence, and the adequacy of elicited probabilities as behavioral measurements. Those assumptions require validation, including conditions where the reference model is deliberately misspecified. No continuous distortion scores have been estimated in the existing experiments. The earlier 0.1 percent versus 26.9 percent result is a separate, binary comparison with a specific confidence-weighted rule. The conceptual ancestry lies in classical work on belief aggregation and social inference rather than in a newly invented mathematical primitive (DeGroot, 1974; Golub & Jackson, 2010; Bikhchandani et al., 1992).","kind":"hypothesis"}],"sources":["degroot1974reaching","golub2010naive","bikhchandani1992theory"]},{"id":"early-prediction","label":"Measurement versus prediction","act":"Part III · The proposed science","heading":"A predictor must work from early messages without the experimenter's full knowledge","duration":107,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"A scientific explanation can use information that becomes available only after an experiment ends. The experimenter knows which observations were assigned, what they signaled, and what the correct answer was. That knowledge supports reference calculations and tests of the proposed distortion construct. A deployed predictor cannot rely on the same privileges. This is why the program separates measuring a failure retrospectively from forecasting it while a network is still exchanging messages.","kind":"hypothesis"},{"at":28.1,"text":"The forecasting point would be fixed at least two rounds before the final judgment. At that point the predictor could use the observed message history, sender and receiver traces, reported confidence, current group belief, and known configuration. It could use a feature estimator trained to approximate aspects of distortion from such observable traces, but it could not receive the oracle update, the true answer, or the terminal belief as inputs. Those later quantities may define training targets or evaluation outcomes. Splits must be constructed so that later rounds and duplicated instances cannot leak into training or into held-out evaluation through identifiers or shared construction procedures.","kind":"hypothesis"},{"at":68.1,"text":"The relevant comparison is not simply whether a complicated predictor classifies failures better than chance. It must outperform strong baselines using the same decision-time information, including connectivity, message type, current collective belief, and models using the identical traces without proposed distortion features. Discrimination alone is insufficient. A warning stated as a probability must be calibrated, and any policy that abstains rather than answers must be evaluated at an explicit coverage level. If distortion features fail to improve prediction under these conditions, they may remain useful explanatory diagnostics, but they would not support the program's central transfer claim.","kind":"hypothesis"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"neighborhood","label":"Research context","act":"Part III · The proposed science","heading":"Adjacent DARPA programs study agent communication with different measures","duration":87,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"The proposed questions sit near several ongoing DARPA efforts, though the scientific case for CEA does not depend on a categorical claim that no other program studies errors. MATHBAC, in the Defense Sciences Office, seeks mathematical theory about communication among agents and its consequences for scientific discovery, with examinations of success, speed, and generalization (Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency, 2026). DICE, in the Information Processing Techniques Office, develops decentralized coordination and local inference control for resilient missions. It explicitly considers how agents integrate contradictory evidence and how errors or corrupted information propagate (Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency, 2026). SIQ addresses prediction of AI-model performance in scenarios not yet evaluated (Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency, 2026).","kind":"schematic"},{"at":43.6,"text":"CEA's proposed object is narrower than general coordination and different from model-level performance prediction. It is the occurrence of confidently wrong collective judgments on tasks with a known answer, and especially whether their risk can be predicted in held-out combinations of communication graph, model, and task. The planned examination also asks whether controls improve decision accuracy rather than merely suppress stated confidence or reduce the fraction of cases answered. The published program materials establish differences in stated objectives and evaluation criteria, not exclusive research boundaries. The overlap with DICE on evidence integration and with MATHBAC on communication theory is part of the research context, not something to conceal.","kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["darpa2026mathbac","darpa2026dice","darpa2026siq"]},{"id":"gate","label":"Month-22 gate","act":"Part IV · How the central claim could fail","heading":"The central test is prediction in configurations the predictor never saw","duration":101,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"The decisive examination is proposed for month 22. From message and configuration information available at least two rounds before a collective decision, a predictor would estimate the risk that the final judgment will be confidently wrong. For this program's examination, confidently wrong means placing at least 0.8 probability on the incorrect answer. A forecast made after seeing the terminal belief would be a description, not this kind of prediction.","kind":"hypothesis"},{"at":27.4,"text":"Independent evaluators would test three kinds of shift separately: communication graphs not used for development, participating model families not used for development, and one limited, predeclared task family withheld from development. The provisional targets within each shift family are at least 0.10 absolute AUROC improvement over the strongest relevant baseline, expected calibration error below 0.05, and coverage of at least 80 percent. The evaluation would need to prespecify the confidence bins or estimation procedure for calibration, the baseline selection rule, uncertainty around differences, and how abstention is handled. These are proposed standards for a future test, not performance levels the existing studies have achieved.","kind":"hypothesis"},{"at":67.1,"text":"A result that transfers across graphs and models but fails on the new task family would still teach us something about the boundary of the phenomenon. It would not establish the general claim being proposed. The program would not move automatically into its original Phase III objectives after failure in any required family. Passing the gate would be evidence for useful predictive transport under the specified tests, not a proof that the predictor will work in every application or every future model.","kind":"hypothesis"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"success","label":"Month-36 evaluation","act":"Part IV · How the central claim could fail","heading":"An intervention has to improve decisions, not only appear cautious","duration":100,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"If prediction survives the transfer gate, the next question is whether that knowledge can change outcomes. Possible controls include carrying provenance information with messages, limiting routes that repeatedly expose an agent to the same source, or selectively rechecking a judgment before it is relayed. These interventions act at different points in the process. Provenance tags help a recipient identify dependence. Routing changes exposure. Rechecking changes the opportunity to correct an emerging conclusion. Their relative effects should be examined rather than assumed.","kind":"hypothesis"},{"at":31.6,"text":"At the proposed month-36 examination, controls would face blind source duplication, message corruption, model updates, and task shifts. Success would require halving confidently wrong judgments relative to both no control and source tracking alone, while holding coverage and latency comparable. It would also require a gain of at least five percentage points in decision accuracy and an improvement in log score. Lowering confidence on wrong answers may improve calibration, but it does not satisfy the accuracy requirement if the decisions remain wrong. Refusing to answer more cases cannot be credited as success when the coverage floor is binding.","kind":"hypothesis"},{"at":69.2,"text":"The current planning envelope is roughly $15 million to $25 million over 36 months, with three or four performer teams and independent evaluation. These are illustrative design assumptions. No funds, transition commitment, or performer relationships are implied. The science and any eventual transition would be evaluated separately. A successful experimental measure is not automatically an operationally acceptable intervention, particularly if it depends on unobservable provenance, excessive computational expense, or delayed access to correct answers.","kind":"hypothesis"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"closing","label":"The question","act":"Part IV · How the central claim could fail","heading":"The claim worth testing is whether early signatures predict error in unfamiliar systems","duration":89,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"The work presented here establishes that communication choices can change collective accuracy, that the resulting errors are not adequately described as uniform false consensus, and that one early planted verdict can move a group's terminal belief under controlled conditions. It leaves open the process by which received messages acquire effective weight and whether that process can be measured in a way that generalizes.","kind":"hypothesis"},{"at":25.2,"text":"CEA would put that uncertainty under examination. The proposed construct would first have to survive tests of its own measurement assumptions. Features derived from early messages would then have to predict confident collective error in held-out graphs, models, and tasks more accurately and more reliably than simpler alternatives. Finally, controls informed by those predictions would have to improve actual decisions at comparable coverage and cost. A predictor that only works on familiar tasks would support a narrower claim. A predictor that adds nothing beyond simpler traces would fail the proposed transfer test.","kind":"hypothesis"},{"at":60.7,"text":"The reason to study the problem at the level of a communicating system is that the evidence available to a collective and the judgment it ultimately makes are connected by a process, not by a simple sum. The question is whether enough of that process can be observed early to say when reliance on the eventual judgment is warranted. That answer remains to be established.","kind":"hypothesis"}],"sources":[]}]};
